{{!-- =====================================================
     PROJECT EXPERIENCE EVIDENCE REVIEW
===================================================== --}}

<main class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">


    {{!-- =================================================
         INTRODUCTION
    ================================================== --}}

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        PROJECT EXPERIENCE
      </p>

      <h1>
        Project Experience Evidence Review
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Don't simply choose projects that look similar.
        Review the evidence Phil provides against the RFP,
        the client's priorities, and the pursuit strategy to
        determine which experience makes the strongest case
        for selection.
      </p>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         QUICK FACTS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="evidence-quick-facts"
    >

      <h2 id="evidence-quick-facts">
        At a Glance
      </h2>

      <div class="sasha-practice-fact-grid">


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            PURPOSE
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            Find the strongest proof
          </p>

          <p>
            Determine which completed projects and which specific
            evidence from those projects best support the case you
            are making in this pursuit.
          </p>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            PHIL'S ROLE
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            Produce the evidence
          </p>

          <p>
            Phil finds and develops relevant project experience,
            including comparable work, challenges, services,
            outcomes, metrics, lessons learned, and other proof
            of past capability.
          </p>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            SASHA'S ROLE
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            Test the evidence
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha helps evaluate Phil's evidence against the
            specific pursuit and determine where it strengthens
            the proposal argument.
          </p>

        </article>


      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         PHIL + SASHA
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="evidence-roles"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            TWO DIFFERENT JOBS
          </p>

          <h2 id="evidence-roles">
            Phil knows the projects. Sasha knows the pursuit.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Phil preserves and develops the firm's evidence of
            completed project experience. He helps find projects
            with comparable scope, challenges, technologies,
            delivery conditions, clients, disciplines, outcomes,
            and other useful characteristics.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha should not duplicate that work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha's job is to take the evidence Phil provides and
            ask a different question: which of these examples best
            proves that this team can successfully deliver what
            this client needs now?
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         EVIDENCE IS NOT A PROJECT LIST
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="evidence-proof"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            START WITH PROOF
          </p>

          <h2 id="evidence-proof">
            Relevant experience is more than a project list.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p>
          A project name, client name, contract value, or impressive
          photograph may establish that the firm performed the work.
          It does not necessarily prove the argument the proposal
          needs to make.
        </p>

        <p>
          Look inside each project for evidence of capability.
        </p>


        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Comparable scope and services</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Similar technical challenges</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Comparable project conditions</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Problems successfully solved</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Risks successfully managed</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Measurable results and outcomes</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Innovations or improvements</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Lessons that apply to this assignment</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         REVIEW PROCESS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="evidence-process"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            CONDUCT THE REVIEW
          </p>

          <h2 id="evidence-process">
            Test each example against the pursuit.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-step-list">


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            01
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Start with the RFP
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify what the client actually asks the proposal
              to demonstrate. Pay particular attention to evaluation
              criteria, relevant experience requirements, mandatory
              qualifications, scope, and stated project challenges.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            02
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Consider the win strategy
            </h3>

            <p>
              Review the client's priorities, known concerns,
              competitive position, win themes, differentiators,
              and the arguments the proposal needs to prove.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            03
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Ask Phil for the evidence
            </h3>

            <p>
              Use Phil to identify completed projects with
              comparable problems, conditions, services, outcomes,
              technologies, delivery methods, or other characteristics
              that matter to this pursuit.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            04
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Look beyond similarity
            </h3>

            <p>
              A project does not need to be identical to be powerful
              evidence. Determine whether the team successfully solved
              a comparable problem under comparable conditions.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            05
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Identify what it proves
            </h3>

            <p>
              State the proposition supported by the evidence.
              Does the example prove technical capability, schedule
              control, stakeholder management, constructability,
              innovation, risk management, cost control, or another
              important capability?
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            06
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Look for outcomes
            </h3>

            <p>
              Strong evidence shows what happened because of the
              team's work. Look for measurable results, successful
              decisions, risks avoided, efficiencies achieved,
              improvements delivered, and client outcomes.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            07
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Rank the evidence
            </h3>

            <p>
              Decide which examples provide the clearest and most
              persuasive proof. The most recognizable or largest
              project is not automatically the strongest example.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            08
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Decide where to use it
            </h3>

            <p>
              Connect the selected evidence to the parts of the
              proposal it strengthens: relevant experience,
              methodology, project understanding, risk management,
              win themes, executive messaging, or other evaluated
              content.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         SIMILARITY
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="evidence-similarity"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            RELEVANCE
          </p>

          <h2 id="evidence-similarity">
            Similar does not mean identical.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Clients rarely procure an exact copy of a project the
            firm has already completed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strong evidence may come from a project in another
            location, sector, discipline, or delivery environment
            if it demonstrates that the firm has successfully solved
            the same kind of problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Review the underlying challenge, constraints, risks,
            decisions, services, and outcomes — not just the project
            category.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         QUESTIONS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="evidence-questions"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
          QUESTIONS TO ASK
        </p>

        <h2 id="evidence-questions">
          Make every example earn its place.
        </h2>


        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              What does this prove?
            </strong>

            <span>
              Identify the capability or promise that the example
              substantiates.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Why will the client care?
            </strong>

            <span>
              Connect the evidence to an evaluation criterion,
              concern, objective, risk, or desired outcome.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              What is truly comparable?
            </strong>

            <span>
              Look for similar challenges, constraints, services,
              conditions, decisions, or outcomes.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              What was accomplished?
            </strong>

            <span>
              Prefer evidence that demonstrates successful delivery
              rather than simply participation.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Can we quantify it?
            </strong>

            <span>
              Look for credible metrics, quantities, schedule
              performance, savings, improvements, or other
              measurable results.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Where should we use it?
            </strong>

            <span>
              Determine which proposal argument becomes stronger
              when this evidence is attached to it.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WARNING SIGNS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="evidence-warning"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WATCH FOR THESE
          </p>

          <h2 id="evidence-warning">
            Evidence can look impressive and still be weak.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">×</span>
            <span>Project lists without explanation</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">×</span>
            <span>Big project names with unclear relevance</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">×</span>
            <span>Examples selected only because the scope looks similar</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">×</span>
            <span>Technical competence without client outcomes</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">×</span>
            <span>Claims without supporting examples</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">×</span>
            <span>Generic evidence that could support any proposal</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WORKING WITH PHIL AND SASHA
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="evidence-assistants"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WORKING WITH PHIL + SASHA
          </p>

          <h2 id="evidence-assistants">
            Find the evidence with Phil. Test it with Sasha.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Ask Phil to find and develop project evidence that
            appears relevant to the opportunity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bring that evidence back into the pursuit and work
            with Sasha to compare it against the RFP, client
            priorities, evaluation criteria, project challenges,
            win strategy, and proposed methodology.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha can then help determine which evidence should
            support which proposal arguments — without replacing
            Phil's role as the firm's source of project experience.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           WHAT TO LOOK FOR
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          What to look for during the review
        </h2>

        <p>
          The purpose of the review is not simply to confirm
          that project experience exists. It is to determine
          whether the available evidence is strong enough,
          specific enough, and relevant enough to support
          the pursuit.
        </p>

        <p>
          As you review the evidence with Sasha, consider:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            <strong>Relevance.</strong>
            Does the project experience closely match the
            client's project, scope, environment, or challenge?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Specificity.</strong>
            Does the evidence describe what the firm actually
            did, rather than relying on broad claims?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Results.</strong>
            Are there measurable outcomes, improvements,
            efficiencies, savings, schedule achievements,
            or other results?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Client value.</strong>
            Does the evidence explain why the work mattered
            to the client?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Similarity.</strong>
            Are there meaningful parallels between the past
            project and the current pursuit?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Differentiation.</strong>
            Does the evidence demonstrate an advantage,
            capability, method, or result that competitors
            may have difficulty matching?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Credibility.</strong>
            Is the evidence concrete enough to withstand
            scrutiny by an evaluator?
          </li>

        </ul>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           LOOK FOR GAPS
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Look for gaps — not just strengths
        </h2>

        <p>
          A good evidence review should also reveal where
          the proposal team does <strong>not</strong> yet
          have the evidence it needs.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sasha can help identify claims or win themes that
          appear important to the pursuit but are not yet
          supported by strong project evidence.
        </p>

        <p>
          A gap might mean that the team needs to:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            ask Phil for additional project experience;
          </li>

          <li>
            search for a more relevant project;
          </li>

          <li>
            obtain stronger results or metrics;
          </li>

          <li>
            confirm details with a project manager;
          </li>

          <li>
            find evidence from another office, discipline,
            or business unit; or
          </li>

          <li>
            reconsider a proposal claim that cannot be
            adequately supported.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          Finding these gaps during planning is much better
          than discovering them while the proposal is being
          written.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           CONNECT EVIDENCE TO THE WIN STRATEGY
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Connect the evidence to the win strategy
        </h2>

        <p>
          Strong project evidence should not sit separately
          from the proposal strategy. It should reinforce it.
        </p>

        <p>
          For each important proposal argument, ask:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            What are we trying to convince the client of?
          </li>

          <li>
            What project experience proves that we can
            deliver it?
          </li>

          <li>
            What specific fact, result, or example makes
            that proof credible?
          </li>

          <li>
            Where should that evidence appear in the
            proposal?
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          Sasha can help make those connections so that
          evidence gathered through Phil becomes useful
          proposal proof rather than simply background
          information.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           PHIL AND SASHA
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Phil finds the evidence. Sasha helps you use it.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Phil remains the firm's project experience
          specialist. His job is to locate, develop, and
          provide reliable project evidence from the firm's
          experience.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sasha's role begins when that evidence is brought
          into the pursuit.
        </p>

        <p>
          She helps the proposal team examine it in the
          context of the client, the RFP, the win strategy,
          and the arguments the proposal must make.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            The distinction is important:
          </strong>

          <p>
            Phil answers,
            <em>
              "What evidence do we have?"
            </em>
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha helps answer,
            <em>
              "How does this evidence help us win this
              pursuit?"
            </em>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           WHEN TO CONDUCT THE REVIEW
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          When to conduct the evidence review
        </h2>

        <p>
          Conduct the review during proposal planning,
          after the team has developed enough of the win
          strategy to understand the arguments it intends
          to make.
        </p>

        <p>
          Do it early enough that there is still time to
          return to Phil, project managers, or other sources
          when important evidence is missing.
        </p>

        <p>
          The review can also be repeated as the pursuit
          develops. New information from the client,
          addenda, interviews, or strategy discussions may
          change which evidence is most useful.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           PRACTICAL OUTCOME
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          What you should have when the review is complete
        </h2>

        <p>
          At the end of the review, the proposal team should
          have a clear understanding of:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            the strongest project evidence available for
            the pursuit;
          </li>

          <li>
            which proposal arguments that evidence supports;
          </li>

          <li>
            the specific facts and results worth carrying
            into the proposal;
          </li>

          <li>
            where important evidence gaps remain; and
          </li>

          <li>
            what additional information should be requested
            from Phil or the project team.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          That gives the writers something much more useful
          than a collection of project descriptions. It
          gives them evidence selected deliberately to
          support the proposal's strategy.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           RETURN TO PLAN
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-actions">

        <a
          class="sasha-practice-back-link"
          href="/plan{{#if pursuitId}}?pursuit={{pursuitId}}{{/if}}"
        >
          Return to Plan
        </a>

      </div>

    </div>

  </main>

</div>